description.narrative:
The Central South regions of Niger recorded rare rainfall of several hundred mm between August 30 and September 6, 2024, causing significant damage in 2,121 localities in Niger (Directorate of Preparedness, Alert and Disaster Management of the Ministry of Humanitarian Action - 04 Sept 2024). 51,685 households with 378,569 people are affected with 69 deaths, at least 640 heads of livestock lost, 10,623 ha of crops destroyed, 335 drinking water points damaged, 1,541 latrines collapsed. The most severely affected regions areMaradi (22,456 people or 2,692 households), Dosso (21,896 people or 2,782 households), Zinder (19,467 people or 2,561 households) and Tillabéry (10,617 people or 1,491 households). This will increase the vulnerability and exposure of vulnerable groups such as women, girls, children under 5, the elderly, those with special needs (chronic diseases) and those living with disabilities. Indeed, the floods have led to massive displacement of these vulnerable groups to take shelter in a combination of sordid conditions of food and nutrition insecurity, loss of medicines, difficult access to basic social services, water, sanitation and hygiene facilities, thus exposing them to an epidemic of water-borne diseases such as cholera as well as a peak or resurgence of vector-borne diseases suchas malaria, Rift Valley fever with ecological conditions favorable to the proliferation of vectors. In Zinder, a rapid need analysis conducted by Oxfam and confirmed by official data from the Ministry of Humanitarian Action and Disaster Management highlights significant gaps/shortfalls not yet covered in the response to floods in this region. Thus, as of September 9, 2024, 19,467 people (2,561 households) have not yet been assisted (Needs Matrix 9 Sept 2024 - OCHA). The needs of 2,741 shelters, 2,741 non-food items, 256,1tons of cereals, 58.4 tons of legumes, 25.34 tons of salt, 50,685 liters of oil, identified have not yet been met. Note that for food items, these are monthly needs. However, in such circumstances, it is advisable to provide assistance for at least three months. Lack of response by humanitarian actors contributes to reinforcing the disarray of vulnerable groups and the decapitalization of their assets, particularly small ruminants, some of which have already been lost in the floods. Finally, a summary market analysis by our teams indicates that the latter remain functional even if the inhabitants of certain villages, due to the impassability of the roads, encounter difficulties in accessing them. It should be noted that the amount of the official Minimum Expenditure Basket (MEB) in Niger is CAD 74.55. However, according to WFP assessments for the Zinder region (September 2024), an amount of CAD 91.75 is necessary to cover the minimum basket. Oxfam's action will be coordinated with the recommendations of the Cash Working Group (CWG) and aligned with the determine value of transfer.
Oxfam in Niger plans to conduct a multi-sectoral response (WASH, Multi-Purpose Cash Assistance (MPCA) and Protection) to help meet the acute humanitarian needs of flood victims in the communes of Dakoussa and Trimini (Zinder region) in accordance with its 2024 flood response plan, which is aligned with the strategic objectives of the Niger Government's 2024 Flood Response Plan. This action aims to save lives and alleviate the suffering of the flood-affected populations in the villages of Aroungouza, Angoual Tsamia and Kangarawa (Dakoussa) and Tchantcharwa and Maikombarawa (Tirmini), which are the most affectedin these communes. Of the 19,467 people affected by the floods in this region, the Canadian funds requested (CHAF) will help approximately 27% of vulnerable people.

In this response, principal activities will be:
- 2 unconditional cash transferts (vouchers), with value of 60 000 FCFA (137.69 CAD), including food assistance (91.75 CAD) and NFI (45.55 CAD) for 664 vulnerable households (5,275 people) to complement the monthly support intendend to be provided by the WFP and the crisis modifier from the projet DGD Developpement of Oxfam (complementarity which will allowe us to cover the recommended 3-month support) ;
- Construction of emergency latrines and/or construction and rehabilitation of latrines;
- Distribution of home water purification products;
- Mass awareness raising on essential family practices and the prevention of dirty hands and/or vector-borne diseases such as cholera, malaria, etc.;
- Distribution of dignity kits;
- Awareness raising on protection and available protection services as well as counseling support for survivors.

Oxfam is targeting the municipalities of Dakoussa and Trimini, where we have been conducting development actions for several years with funding from the DGD-Development. This is what allowed us to conduct a rapid analysis to assess the impact of the floods on the direct beneficiaries of this project. This, combined with the presence of a base in Zinder, will allow us to be operational veryquickly and reduce operating costs and indirect costs so that the funding can go largely and as a priority to the victims. Also forbetter coordination, Oxfam, which is already taking part in regional coordination meetings for advocacy and fundraising in responseto the humanitarian crisis in Zinder, will share its planning and, if necessary, consult with any potential actors who have positioned themselves in the municipality to reduce the risks of duplication of assistance. This response to the Oxfam floods in Niger willbe focused on the affected people and in consultation with all stakeholders (Humanitarian Affait Ministry, Authorities, Community Leaders, OCS, present humanitarian actors, etc.) in the target area. For this, a participatory and inclusive approach will be adoptedat all levels following a preliminary mappingof the actors already present and the actions in progress as well as those who are positioning themselves, for better coordination for complementarity and synergy in the response to the floods.

To adapt the intervention to the differentiated needs related to gender, age and the specific needs of certain people, a rapid analysis will be quickly conducted to ensure that the response remains sensitive to the context and takes into consideration the endogenous understandings, capacities and know-how of thevictims in their diversity.
It should be noted that although humanitarian actors have not started to deploy on the ground, some actors such as NGO Goal, UNICEF and WFP have positioned themselves in the Zinder region mainly to provide assistance in non-food items and cash for food for one month. While UNFPA has positioned itself at the national level for rape kits. Ouraction aims to ensure synergy and temporal or sectoral complementarity with the planning and actions of these actors through active, iterative and regular consultations and discussions.
Oxfam has been working in Niger since 1992 and has had continuous representation in the Zinder region for over a decade, leading to the establishment of a base over three years ago. Currently, Oxfam is implementing a development project there with funding from DGD Belgium, in which, due to the fragility of the area and the recurrence of natural shocks, a crisis modification mechanism has been planned to preserve the gains made against these contingencies.Moreover, we are targeting the municipalities of Dakoussa and Timini, to enable us to have the financial capacity to cover all households affectedby flooding in your intervention villages in order to avoid the risk of finding or frustration that could arise from exclusive support for beneficiaries of the DGD Development project through the crisis modifier (complementarity). To implement this project, Oxfamwill work in partnership with the NGO Garkua. NGO Garkua is a national NGO that has worked with Oxfamin Niger for around four yearsin both development and humanitarian sectors. This partnership facilitates community engagement, acceptance of Oxfam and its partners as well as the localization of our interventions taking into account local capacities andopportunities. Garkua has proven capacities in the field of cash transfers, but also in food security and protection. The organization has a solid base in the Zinder Regionand an ongoing funding agreement with Oxfam in Niger, for the Zinder Region (as part of the DGD Development Project on which the activation of the "crisis modifier" is triggered to support the response to this flood crisis). Oxfam has proven capacities in the conduct of cash transfers including evaluation tools (feasibility of Cash, Market Monitoring) and currently has a framework contract for cash transfers with a national money transfer agency named NITA. Oxfam in Niger has implemented and continues to implement Cash Transfer Projects with funding from various donors such as ECHO, GFFO, SIDA, DANIDA, AECID, etc.

In NIger, Oxfam and its partners intervene in all vital sectors, particularly Food and Nutritional security, Water, Sanitation and Hygiene, and emergency Protection, inconflict-sensitive approaches to reduce the risk ofexacerbating conflicts and a single program (One Program approach) combining life-saving activities with advocacy for the protection and promotion of human rights. We also collaborate with local authorities, technical services as well as local service providers and suppliers to whom we are linked, for some, by framework contracts facilitatingthe rapid acquisition of goods and services. Also, in order to give itself the chance to conduct activities safely without harming and exposing staff or the reputation of the institution, Oxfam in Niger adopts an approach in a safe programming approach. Oxfam in Niger also has security and prevention policies with standard operating procedures to reduce security risks and preserve the dignityof the people targeted byour intervention. In a concern for accountability to the various stakeholders, Oxfam in Niger, as part of these interventions, sets up local complaint management mechanisms called complaint management committees whose members chosen by their peers invillageassemblies are trained and equipped to play the role assigned to them. In addition, a toll-free number is provided to communities to file their complaints or report any suspicion of reprehensible behavior on the part of Oxfam staff, their colleaguesorpartners. With qualified and experienced staff in all the areas mentioned above, Oxfam and its partners have extensive experience in emergency response throughout the country."
